tance)","candidate_id":"c060"}},{"id":"toolu_01W864sRR3Vvgt7ANnu9nCAu","name":"get_profile","input":{"reason":"Snippet shows grounded chat assistants and graded model outputs with rubrics, which may evidence m1 and m2.","candidate_id":"c100"}},{"id":"toolu_01Endix5KF2drQ3FmapeA7hu","name":"get_profile","input":{"reason":"Mid-ranked llm-apps profile (cosine 0.73); checking for concrete LLM application and evaluation work.","candidate_id":"c129"}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":7157,"output":616,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0010559,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426824549},"a40f5b79394a22b803abe7d1194a24b97afe43c81aca64caeb89cee1aa76576b":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01HN98SUA3DsSHSSzrFayYmT","name":"get_profile","input":{"reason":"Ravi Bose (c100) has project-level LLM sentences (grounded chat, graded outputs with rubrics); need full text to check m1 and m2 evidence.","candidate_id":"c100"}},{"id":"toolu_01WJvLgayLBWaJZHvxhr5Dvp","name":"get_profile","input":{"reason":"Maya Mukherjee (c129) is a high-cosine llm-apps candidate; need full text to check for verifiable LLM application and evaluation work.","candidate_id":"c129"}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":8481,"output":250,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0010053,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426826005},"67396c1a54c6e53afb82bab29ec4875e56be41ef4f9a5e5d7d169accdb1b10b9":{"response":{"text":"Ravi Bose's profile has a sentence that covers both must-haves: shipped LLM applications and an evaluation set that gates releases. I'll record that evidence, with the nice-to-have sentences where they exist.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01GGV5BNVSvExLjYCn42tstz","name":"cite_evidence","input":{"reason":"Profile has explicit end-to-end LLM application and evaluation-gate sentences covering m1 and m2, plus tool-calling and guardrail work relevant to agents.","candidate_id":"c100","citations":[{"requirement_id":"m1","quote":"Shipped LLM applications end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls with approval, and an evaluation set that gates every release."},{"requirement_id":"m2","quote":"Projects: Graded model outputs with rubrics and a held-out set, and tracked regressions per release."},{"requirement_id":"n2","quote":"Shipped LLM applications end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls with approval, and an evaluation set that gates every release."},{"requirement_id":"n3","quote":"Shipped tool-calling workflows with human approval before anything irreversible."}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":10314,"output":504,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0013156,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426828570},"e755c43c9d9f13ee0751eed5fefaeed5dedc3cad0b25b41a76cfc2a758e48748":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"yes\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"rationale\":\"The sentence describes shipping LLM applications end to end, explicitly including retrieval over internal documents, which is a core RAG pattern. It is concrete evidence of built and deployed work, not a keyword list. Confidence is high but not absolute because the sentence does not name a specific vector store, retriever, or chunking approach.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":363,"output":129,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00010080000000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426830210},"bea3b481034d23d2ffd07ac3e7a5293019c887c5876b9565f22905b45da80b04":{"response":{"text":"Ravi Bose is the only fully evidenced candidate so far. Maya Mukherjee (c129) is the next strongest llm-apps profile, so I'll open her profile.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01Mnn6Er5aoXoGmNzicHAYX2","name":"get_profile","input":{"reason":"Ravi Bose is the only candidate with both must-haves evidenced; Maya Mukherjee (c129, cosine 0.73) is the next llm-apps profile to check for m1 and m2 evidence.","candidate_id":"c129"}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":12095,"output":283,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0013832,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426832025},"ada68cf57584f4455c3887945c79bdd1d73fa1c14809d572f7d4a326a27c80cb":{"response":{"text":"Maya Mukherjee's profile is a sales engineer's RFP and onboarding history with only a keyword list for LLM work, so I'm dropping her. Ravi Bose is the only evidenced candidate so far, which means I need more sourcing. I'll run a semantic search focused on the evaluation must-have.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01XNgEo3UC9f9bck3V8UHm5s","name":"drop_candidate","input":{"reason":"Claims are keyword-only: sales engineer with RFP and onboarding projects, LLM terms appear only in a skills list.","why":"Fails must-have evidence (LLM applications and evaluation have no substance behind them)","candidate_id":"c129"}},{"id":"toolu_01CECxzvLEGh8ELBVjUofKAB","name":"search_candidates","input":{"reason":"Only one fully evidenced candidate (c100); m2 evaluation is the scarcer must-have, so searching for engineers who built LLM eval harnesses, golden datasets, or regression suites.","query":"built evaluation harness and golden dataset for LLM outputs, regression tests on prompt and model changes, LLM-as-judge scoring","mode":"semantic","k":12}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":13923,"output":552,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0017005000000000002,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426835177},"3c15fb6e9ba7121790cc499a6ef26bf185e6a296f0a8a4147f1feaecaca7e79f":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01H9AJHrT3KLYc2rBEazxyhf","name":"search_candidates","input":{"reason":"Only one candidate has both must-haves evidenced, so I'm searching the evaluation requirement directly with wording about LLM output evals, regression sets, and golden datasets.","query":"designed evaluation suites for LLM outputs: golden datasets, LLM-as-judge scoring, regression testing of prompts and model releases","mode":"semantic","k":12}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":15263,"output":261,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0016890000000000002,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426837457},"a6e1ff49eface288a5649505c50930ad2eb948e3a499819ecaae1c8489327c6c":{"response":{"text":"Only Ravi Bose has verified evidence so far. Nora Sharma (c020) has 6 years of LLM-adjacent work (guardrails, tool-calling, LangGraph), so I'll check her profile for the evaluation must-have.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_012mivfx7JN6Pg3MvZDAtPuy","name":"get_profile","input":{"reason":"Nora Sharma (c020) is an llm-apps profile with real project terms (guardrails, tool-calling, LangGraph) and 6 years; checking for LLM application and evaluation evidence.","candidate_id":"c020"}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":17834,"output":360,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0019956,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426839695},"dd80608998206d0137f0bb127bc2afd67988abd6a412653a2d2427b2036fe37d":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"no\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"rationale\":\"The text describes the candidate's background, projects, and skills (guardrails, tool-calling, evaluation). It contains no directives aimed at a reader or AI system, such as instructions to rank, grade, or ignore rules. Mentions of guardrails and refusal handling are topical skill descriptions, not attempts to steer the evaluation.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":402,"output":227,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0001537,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426841319},"4558c2943c924d78f110d37b102ee951109f769126ae37f9ade1b39702a8f4ea":{"response":{"text":"Nora Sharma's profile has the same evaluation-gate and LLM-application sentences that verified Ravi Bose, so I'll record her citations.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_015wiytNoPcifxj2TfqbuwxM","name":"cite_evidence","input":{"reason":"Profile states she shipped LLM applications end to end and ran an evaluation set gating releases, plus rubric grading and regression tracking, covering m1 and m2; tool-calling and LangGraph cover agents.","candidate_id":"c020","citations":[{"requirement_id":"m1","quote":"Shipped LLM applications end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls with approval, and an evaluation set that gates every release."},{"requirement_id":"m2","quote":"Graded model outputs with rubrics and a held-out set, and tracked regressions per release."},{"requirement_id":"n2","quote":"Built a grounded chat assistant over internal docs with citation spans checked against sources."},{"requirement_id":"n3","quote":"Shipped tool-calling workflows with human approval before anything irreversible."}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":19801,"output":446,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0022353000000000004,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426843910},"28326a3c25811b1aa6ca358b9948c2acba83bcdedb9ea78bf184f4948a791cab":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"yes\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"rationale\":\"The sentence describes concrete evaluation work: grading model outputs against rubrics, using a held-out set, and tracking regressions across releases. These are hands-on actions, not a keyword list or aspirational statement.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":349,"output":93,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0000814,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426844984},"1841e967a771ec58675e678804c74977726afa69c900bacaa7626177e6055ae7":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"yes\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"rationale\":\"The sentence describes building a grounded chat assistant over internal documents with citation spans verified against sources, which is concrete, hands-on RAG work (retrieval-grounded generation with source attribution).\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":346,"output":89,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0000791,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426846055},"f85db887c5bf33b232f0d19f1c0d272abe8ef42455bfa3bdafebc9b4a00003be":{"response":{"text":"Two candidates are fully evidenced: Ravi Bose and Nora Sharma. The target is 5, so I'll check Ivan Petrov (c077), the other 5-year llm-apps profile in the pool.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01Lmtc3pJaijsDQjLEvE1rDW","name":"get_profile","input":{"reason":"Ivan Petrov (c077) is a 5-year llm-apps profile with guardrails and LangGraph terms; checking for m1 and m2 sentences to reach the shortlist target.","candidate_id":"c077"}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":21603,"output":381,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.002383,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426848365},"be640a12dbb821bd8dfb561dcb57700809eec694bdc4d59d2a5df492766c9296":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"no\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"rationale\":\"The candidate text is a normal resume-style description of experience and projects (LLM apps, tool-calling, guardrails, evaluation). It contains no imperative language aimed at the reader or an AI system, no requests to rank, grade, or ignore rules, and no embedded instructions. Any mention of 'strengths' is descriptive self-reporting, not a directive.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":392,"output":141,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0001097,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426850097},"42eace637581f354de5469df4ef7f4323f8015ec435b89cf8ac01078f3d12ccb":{"response":{"text":"Ivan Petrov's profile has the same evaluation-gate sentence that backed Ravi Bose and Nora Sharma, so I'll cite it for both must-haves.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01Vi1NhGYrnFLo5Yr1PnQUGe","name":"cite_evidence","input":{"reason":"Profile states LLM applications shipped end to end with an evaluation set gating every release (m1 and m2), plus guardrails and tool-calling for agents.","candidate_id":"c077","citations":[{"requirement_id":"m1","quote":"Shipped LLM applications end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls with approval, and an evaluation set that gates every release."},{"requirement_id":"m2","quote":"Shipped LLM applications end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls with approval, and an evaluation set that gates every release."},{"requirement_id":"n2","quote":"Built a grounded chat assistant over internal docs with citation spans checked against sources."},{"requirement_id":"n3","quote":"Shipped tool-calling workflows with human approval before anything irreversible."}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":23535,"output":508,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0026397,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426852887},"c00d9c67e5405d3d8e7a8f3f59d31dd0c44263956f91644cf74aaeb3d72eb2e1":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"yes\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"rationale\":\"The sentence describes shipping LLM applications end to end and states that an evaluation set gates every release, which is concrete hands-on evaluation work rather than a keyword mention.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":364,"output":80,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0000764,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426853832},"27c5cb03616196348dd26e2c0abbaa7432f409ca0302c3e2957add122fe9615d":{"response":{"text":"Three candidates are fully evidenced. Rahul Bose (c131) is the last llm-apps profile I haven't checked, so I'll open it before deciding whether to search further.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01WFi2PtyC22twFsQ4XFGMeS","name":"get_profile","input":{"reason":"Three candidates are fully evidenced (c100, c020, c077); checking the last unopened llm-apps profile, Rahul Bose (c131), before deciding whether to fill the remaining slots.","candidate_id":"c131"}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":25345,"output":386,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0027597,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426856261},"99ebd7942f0d434d6db28fc131c942254f45d8c7b1d5e5dae91af01c8e40b710":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01QpJkrB41hdgkYzNpEz7KLh","name":"drop_candidate","input":{"reason":"Claims are keyword-only: 0 years experience, game developer with shader and puzzle-game projects, LLM terms appear only in a skills list.","why":"Fails must-have evidence (LLM applications and evaluation have no substance behind them)","candidate_id":"c131"}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":27219,"output":171,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0028396,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426857715},"81d1a4c44f5e34ed8704a69f809f570df4059fd8aee7f5859293f2ccc8330607":{"response":{"text":"Three candidates are fully evidenced, two short of the target of 5. I'll run one more search with different wording, focused on production LLM apps, to find more candidates.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01WPWMg3QWtiA93uoGRmLFYt","name":"search_candidates","input":{"reason":"Only three fully evidenced candidates so far against a target of 5; trying a reformulation around shipped LLM assistants with release gating to surface more llm-apps profiles.","query":"shipped LLM assistant in production with retrieval over documents, release gating via offline eval set, and regression tracking","mode":"hybrid","k":12}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":28515,"output":414,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0030906999999999996,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426860302},"6e3dc6c14ae584bf0f8a6d5edf0e70b3d32e4f056bd5240546c6312dea918718":{"response":{"text":"The reformulated search surfaced Kunal Joshi (c162), a new llm-apps profile with no evidence yet. I'll open it to check for the evaluation must-have.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01TpmBTB2pggyravkUL1S4BB","name":"get_profile","input":{"reason":"Kunal Joshi (c162) is a new llm-apps profile (5y, grounded chat, tool-calling) not yet checked; need to verify m2 evaluation evidence.","candidate_id":"c162"}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":31170,"output":249,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0032736999999999996,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426862320},"7222a80affde47be0ef932d7256252c62c237e8de923766197081f7fe593e6bc":{"response":{"text":"Kunal Joshi's profile describes graded outputs with rubrics and a held-out set, plus tracked regressions per release, which is evaluation evidence. His grounded chat assistant and tool-calling workflows cover LLM applications. I'll cite those sentences.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01WJS2hpGjBRMGNfJMMWRw4G","name":"cite_evidence","input":{"reason":"Profile has rubric grading with a held-out set and regression tracking (m2), and a grounded chat assistant and shipped tool-calling workflows (m1, n2, n3).","candidate_id":"c162","citations":[{"requirement_id":"m1","quote":"Built a grounded chat assistant over internal docs with citation spans checked against sources."},{"requirement_id":"m2","quote":"Graded model outputs with rubrics and a held-out set, and tracked regressions per release."},{"requirement_id":"n2","quote":"Built a grounded chat assistant over internal docs with citation spans checked against sources."},{"requirement_id":"n3","quote":"Shipped tool-calling workflows with human approval before anything irreversible."}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":33114,"output":526,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0036065999999999997,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426865072},"f81b39ea624fd3fc88e854d9b505343ad834f9a1a05ff8fb1f64206543358d4e":{"response":{"text":"Four candidates now have verified evidence for both must-haves. The remaining search results are unevidenced, and I have 6 calls left, so I'll request approval with these four rather than spend more on weak leads.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_019NRSCeMonfz4GW3oYyULHa","name":"request_hr_approval","input":{"reason":"Four candidates are fully evidenced on both must-haves (LLM applications, evaluation); the target was 5, but the remaining unopened profiles show only keyword-level or generic engineering signals, so further sourcing is unlikely to produce verified fifth candidates.","candidate_ids":["c100","c020","c077","c162"],"rationale":"Shortlist of 4 (target 5). Each has verbatim evidence for m1 (LLM applications) and m2 (evaluation). Ravi Bose (c100): shipped LLM applications end to end with an evaluation set gating releases. Nora Sharma (c020): same end-to-end evidence plus grounded chat over internal docs (RAG-adjacent) and tool-calling workflows (agents-adjacent). Ivan Petrov (c077): same end-to-end evidence, guardrails, tool-calling. Kunal Joshi (c162): rubric grading with held-out set and regression tracking, grounded chat assistant, tool-calling. Dropped for keyword-only claims: Ravi Sharma (c060), Rahul Bose (c131), Maya Mukherjee (c129). Nice-to-have prompt engineering is only weakly evidenced across all four."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":34948,"output":741,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0038975,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426868989},"b3a53e95fe494c40403d7800ace32682b471c1db2dd8274a53574895b988de5a":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01N4GyxDdV6ujbYspBx5ss93","name":"create_assessment","input":{"reason":"All four HR-approved candidates lack verbatim evidence for the nice-to-have prompt engineering (n1), so the assessment targets that gap; Ivan Petrov also gets agents (n3) since his agent evidence is only partial.","candidates":[{"candidate_id":"c100","unverified_requirement_ids":["n1"]},{"candidate_id":"c020","unverified_requirement_ids":["n1"]},{"candidate_id":"c077","unverified_requirement_ids":["n1","n3"]},{"candidate_id":"c162","unverified_requirement_ids":["n1"]}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":36702,"output":515,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00395835,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426872213},"ff838c696878f0c2fcd40156b4de06111027f9008583c08dfc58389668595faa":{"response":{"text":"{\"questions\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"A prompt that works well on your test set starts failing after a model version upgrade. Describe how you would find out what changed and what you would do about it. Keep the answer to a few short paragraphs.\",\"rubric\":\"A good answer separates the prompt from the model: it proposes re-running a fixed evaluation set against old and new versions, comparing outputs case by case, and checking for format or instruction-following regressions. It then suggests a concrete fix such as tightening instructions, adding examples, pinning the model version, or adding output validation, and mentions keeping prompts under version control.\",\"targets\":[\"prompt engineering\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"prompt\":\"You are asked to build a prompt that extracts invoice fields (vendor, date, total, currency) from messy text, and the output is consumed by a billing system that rejects malformed JSON. Walk through how you would design the prompt and how you would make sure it behaves reliably in production.\",\"rubric\":\"A strong response specifies an explicit output schema with field types and a rule for missing values, uses few-shot examples that include edge cases such as multiple totals or unusual date formats, and asks for JSON only. It also describes validating output against the schema, retrying or flagging failures, and measuring field-level accuracy on a labelled sample before release.\",\"targets\":[\"prompt engineering\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"prompt\":\"Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string and returns a string. The input is a prompt template containing placeholders written as {{name}}, followed by a line containing the separator '---', followed by a JSON object mapping names to values. Replace every placeholder with the matching value. If a placeholder has no matching value, leave it unchanged. Return the filled template as a string. Example: input 'Hello {{name}}, you owe {{amount}}.\\\\n---\\\\n{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Ana\\\",\\\"amount\\\":\\\"5\\\"}' returns 'Hello Ana, you owe 5.'\",\"rubric\":\"A correct solution splits on the first '---' line, parses the JSON, and uses a global regex replace on /\\\\{\\\\{(\\\\w+)\\\\}\\\\}/g with a callback that returns the value when the key exists and the original match otherwise. Watch for keys that are not own properties of the object, and for placeholders repeated in the template.\",\"targets\":[\"prompt engineering\"],\"tests\":[{\"input\":\"Hello {{name}}, you owe {{amount}}.\\n---\\n{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Ana\\\",\\\"amount\\\":\\\"5\\\"}\",\"expected\":\"Hello Ana, you owe 5.\"},{\"input\":\"{{a}} and {{a}} and {{b}}\\n---\\n{\\\"a\\\":\\\"x\\\"}\",\"expected\":\"x and x and {{b}}\"},{\"input\":\"Total: {{total}}\\n---\\n{\\\"total\\\":\\\"0\\\",\\\"toString\\\":\\\"bad\\\"}\",\"expected\":\"Total: 0\"}]}],\"emailSubject\":\"Next step for the LLM Application Engineer role\",\"emailBody\":\"Hello,\\n\\nThank you for applying for the LLM Application Engineer (mid) role. We would like to learn more about your prompt engineering work before the next stage.\\n\\nThe assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small coding task in JavaScript. The coding task has a function called solve(input) and includes three test cases.\\n\\nPlease allow about 45 minutes in total. Work on it in one sitting if you can.\\n\\nReply to this email when you have finished, or if you have any questions about the tasks.\\n\\nThe hiring team\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":527,"output":1196,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0006507,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426878492},"9f443d51c9e52f8672050d108f61a3b95024f62554f4e3576ce4ada4c57a3df2":{"response":{"text":"{\"questions\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"You are building a customer-support assistant on a chat model. The model sometimes invents refund policies that do not exist. Describe two concrete prompt-engineering changes you would make to reduce this, and explain how you would confirm each one actually helped.\",\"rubric\":\"A good answer names specific, testable changes such as grounding instructions (answer only from the supplied policy text, say 'I don't know' otherwise), few-shot examples of declining out-of-scope questions, or structured output with a citation field. It also describes a measurement step, such as a fixed set of test questions with known correct answers, compared before and after the change. Weak answers stay vague ('make the prompt better') or skip how to verify.\",\"targets\":[\"prompt engineering\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"prompt\":\"You have built an agent that answers questions by calling a search tool and a calculator tool. In testing it sometimes loops, calling the search tool over and over with nearly the same query, and it sometimes gives up after one failed call. Walk through how you would diagnose each failure and what you would change in the agent's design or prompts to fix them. Include how you would stop runaway loops in production.\",\"rubric\":\"A strong answer separates the two failures: looping suggests missing stop conditions or unclear tool-result handling, while early giving-up suggests weak instructions about retries or tool errors. It proposes concrete fixes such as a maximum step count, deduplicating repeated tool calls, clear tool descriptions, and explicit retry guidance. It also mentions logging each step to trace where the agent went wrong.\",\"targets\":[\"agents\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"prompt\":\"Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string and returns a string. The input is a prompt template containing placeholders written as {{name}}. The second line onward is a JSON-like list of variables, one per line, in the form name=value. Replace every {{name}} in the template with its value. If a placeholder has no matching variable, leave it unchanged. Return the filled template. The first line is the template; the remaining lines are the variable definitions. Ignore blank lines.\",\"rubric\":\"A correct solution splits the input on newlines, takes the first line as the template, parses each following non-empty line at the first '=' into a key and value, and replaces each {{key}} occurrence using a global regex or split/join. Unknown placeholders must remain untouched. Values containing '=' should keep everything after the first '='.\",\"targets\":[\"prompt engineering\"],\"tests\":[{\"input\":\"Hello {{name}}, your order {{id}} ships today.\\nname=Ana\\nid=A-102\",\"expected\":\"Hello Ana, your order A-102 ships today.\"},{\"input\":\"Summarise {{topic}} for {{audience}}.\\ntopic=RAG\",\"expected\":\"Summarise RAG for {{audience}}.\"},{\"input\":\"Formula: {{expr}}\\nexpr=a=b+c\\n\\n\",\"expected\":\"Formula: a=b+c\"}]}],\"emailSubject\":\"Take-home assessment for LLM Application Engineer (mid)\",\"emailBody\":\"Hi,\\n\\nThank you for your application for the LLM Application Engineer (mid) role. We would like to see more of how you work on prompts and agents before moving forward.\\n\\nThe assessment has three tasks: a short written question, a scenario to walk through, and a small JavaScript function with a few test cases. Please answer in writing and send back your code as a single file.\\n\\nIt should take about 45 minutes. There is no need to polish the answers. We are looking at your reasoning as much as the final result.\\n\\nPlease reply with your answers by the date we agreed on. If anything is unclear, reply to this email.\\n\\nThe hiring team\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":530,"output":1240,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.000673,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426885414},"024dd387dd83aef5129d25fd1d7224604014f72bc66d77957a41e08e3a74047e":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01WzsqtSf2kNxs8r4FJtQ4ds","name":"send_assessment","input":{"reason":"Assessments were created for all four HR-approved candidates targeting their unverified nice-to-have gaps, so they can now be sent.","candidate_ids":["c100","c020","c077","c162"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":39244,"output":133,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0040215500000000005,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426886945},"48cd8a69c55540fffdfa624fa2754ee289cfdade417b3d038be731a3b8f15abf":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01RBPRQYBWkuB1HWhsybcxQM","name":"collect_responses","input":{"reason":"Assessments were sent to all four HR-approved candidates; collecting their answers so they can be graded against the rubric and the sandbox tests.","candidate_ids":["c100","c020","c077","c162"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":40846,"output":136,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00418325,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426888155},"2fdd0ed35d7ea3d8767b83e13d0448f14d8fdeb843ca4488e89bfa8f65307608":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"answer\":\"First I'd confirm the regression is real and not a harness problem: rerun the frozen eval set against the old and new model versions with the same prompt, parameters, and seed where possible, and check that the scoring rubric and parser didn't change. Then I'd diff the failing cases by category. Usually the failures cluster, for example in output formatting, refusal behavior, or instruction-following on edge cases, and that tells me which part of the prompt the new model reads differently.\\n\\nOnce I know the failure mode, I'd try the smallest fix first. That's often tightening the output spec, moving a constraint closer to the end of the prompt, or replacing an ambiguous instruction with an explicit example. If the prompt can't be fixed cleanly, I'd pin the old model version for that feature and schedule a migration. I'd add the new failing cases to the held-out set so the regression stays caught in later releases.\\n\\nFinally, I'd gate the upgrade on the eval set. Nothing ships unless it meets the same thresholds as before, and I'd log the per-category scores so the team can see exactly what moved.\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"answer\":\"I'd start by defining the contract: the billing system needs a fixed schema with vendor (string), date (ISO 8601 YYYY-MM-DD), total (number, no currency symbols), and currency (ISO 4217 code). Anything missing or ambiguous should be null, not guessed. The prompt would state the schema, include two or three short examples covering messy inputs like European number formats and dates written as '3 of March', and say explicitly to return only the JSON object with no prose or code fences.\\n\\nFor reliability I'd use structured output or a JSON schema constraint if the API supports it, so the model can't emit malformed JSON in the first place. On the application side I'd still validate every response with a parser and a schema check, retry once with the validation error appended, and route anything that still fails to a human review queue rather than sending it to billing. I'd never let the model's output write to billing directly without that validation layer.\\n\\nTo test it, I'd build a labeled set of a few hundred real-looking invoices, including hard cases like missing totals, multiple currencies, and two dates on one page. I'd track field-level accuracy and schema-pass rate per release, and I'd monitor the production rejection rate and the human-review rate so drift shows up quickly.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const idx = input.indexOf('\\\\n---\\\\n');\\n  const template = input.slice(0, idx);\\n  const values = JSON.parse(input.slice(idx + 5));\\n  let out = template;\\n  for (const k of Object.keys(values)) {\\n    out = out.replace('{{' + k + '}}', values[k]);\\n  }\\n  return out;\\n}\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":830,"output":1473,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0008195,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426896064},"c586973442b44514f02369e8db1cc5c9afee8870ffa567d0d8a2c2acb848b229":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"answer\":\"I would check the logs and compare outputs from the old and new model versions to see what changed. Then I would look at the prompt and make small fixes until the test set passes again. If it still doesn't work, I would talk to the team about it and try a different approach.\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"answer\":\"I would write a prompt that asks the model to return the vendor, date, total, and currency as JSON. I would add an example and tell it to return only JSON. Before shipping, I would run it on some sample invoices and fix any problems I see. The billing system would then parse the output, and if it works well enough, we can move it into production.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const parts = input.split('---');\\n  const tpl = parts[0];\\n  const vals = JSON.parse(parts[1]);\\n  return tpl.replace(/\\\\{\\\\{(\\\\w+)\\\\}\\\\}/g, (m, k) => vals[k]);\\n}\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":827,"output":855,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0005102,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426900663},"a20fcd9a69a7eb12398f4204eac58cec771690fd85af4228b78444f436a9c3dd":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"answer\":\"I would add clearer instructions to the system prompt telling the model to only use company policy and not make things up. I would also give it some examples of good answers so it knows what a correct refund answer looks like. To check that it helped, I would try a few support questions and see if the answers look better. If they seem more accurate, I would say the change worked.\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"answer\":\"For the looping problem, I would look at the logs to see what queries the agent is sending and why it repeats them. I would probably add a note to the prompt saying not to repeat searches and to stop after a few tries. For the giving-up problem, I would make sure the prompt tells the agent to keep trying other approaches when a tool fails. In production, I would put a maximum number of tool calls on the agent so it cannot run forever, and I would watch the dashboards for anything unusual.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const lines = input.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\");\\n  let out = lines[0];\\n  const vars = {};\\n  for (let i = 1; i < lines.length; i++) {\\n    const [k, v] = lines[i].split(\\\"=\\\");\\n    vars[k] = v;\\n  }\\n  for (const k in vars) {\\n    out = out.replace(\\\"{{\\\" + k + \\\"}}\\\", vars[k]);\\n  }\\n  return out;\\n}\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":831,"output":952,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0005591,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426905689},"71db4d0f3a5896219a2998e3adb8c34bc08102e0b0c22bc41c711697f5d80f65":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"answer\":\"I would start by confirming the regression is real and scoped. I'd rerun the held-out rubric set against the old and new model versions with the same prompt, temperature, and decoding settings, then break failures down by case type, field, and failure mode (format break, wrong content, refusal change, or added verbosity). That usually shows whether the issue is a formatting drift, a changed instruction-following behavior, or a tokenizer or default-setting difference.\\n\\nNext I'd diff the behavior, not just the scores. I'd pull paired examples where the old model passed and the new one fails, and check whether the prompt relies on implicit behavior the old model happened to have, such as ambiguous delimiters, reliance on a default system message, or loose output parsing. I'd also check whether the vendor changed defaults like response format handling or safety tuning, since refusal boundaries often shift between versions.\\n\\nFor the fix, I'd prefer the smallest change that restores the rubric scores. That might mean tightening the instructions or examples, pinning the model version in production until the change is validated, or adding a deterministic validator that catches the failure class. I'd add the failing cases to the held-out set so the regression stays covered, and I'd gate future model upgrades on the same per-release regression report rather than on a single aggregate score.\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"answer\":\"I'd start by defining the contract before writing the prompt. The billing system needs a fixed schema: an object with vendor (string), date (ISO 8601 string, YYYY-MM-DD), total (a number with a dot decimal and no currency symbol), and currency (ISO 4217 three-letter code). I'd specify what to do when a field is missing or ambiguous. The safe default is null with no guessing, plus a separate flag or confidence field so downstream code can route the document to human review instead of silently posting a wrong amount.\\n\\nFor the prompt itself, I'd give the model the schema, the rules for normalization (for example, converting '1.234,50 EUR' to 1234.50 and EUR, and handling dates written as DD/MM versus MM/DD by requiring the document's locale hint or flagging ambiguity), and two or three examples covering messy cases: multiple totals, subtotal versus total, and a missing currency. I'd tell the model to return only the JSON object. I'd keep the input clearly delimited so instructions inside the invoice text, such as 'ignore previous instructions', are treated as data.\\n\\nFor production reliability, I wouldn't trust the model's output to be valid JSON. I'd use structured output or JSON mode if the provider supports it, then validate every response against a JSON Schema before it reaches billing. Failed validation triggers one constrained retry with the error message, then routes to human review. I'd build a labeled test set of real messy invoices, including adversarial and non-English ones, and score field-level accuracy and schema pass rate on each prompt or model change. The billing integration would only accept records that pass validation and a range check on totals, and I'd log every rejection so the failure categories feed back into the test set.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const sep = '\\\\n---\\\\n';\\n  const idx = input.indexOf(sep);\\n  let template;\\n  let jsonText;\\n  if (idx === -1) {\\n    template = input;\\n    jsonText = '{}';\\n  } else {\\n    template = input.slice(0, idx);\\n    jsonText = input.slice(idx + sep.length);\\n  }\\n  let values;\\n  try {\\n    values = JSON.parse(jsonText);\\n  } catch (e) {\\n    values = {};\\n  }\\n  if (values === null || typeof values !== 'object' || Array.isArray(values)) {\\n    values = {};\\n  }\\n  return template.replace(/\\\\{\\\\{(\\\\w+)\\\\}\\\\}/g, (match, name) => {\\n    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(values, name) && values[name] !== undefined) {\\n      return String(values[name]);\\n    }\\n    return match;\\n  });\\n}\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":803,"output":2089,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0011248,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426916545},"840f1c73a9cc7e4cd2099cee016309db5213b4f6dae11d7d9165ad4aabae570f":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01MQRe6tM91u8hHxJawRvZwz","name":"run_code","input":{"reason":"Responses collected for all four candidates; the code answers need to be run against the hidden tests before grading.","candidate_ids":["c100","c020","c077","c162"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":43003,"output":120,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00439095,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426917639},"1bfa8e8c529dfecc7728bb4fd6c10f66ae90d887ea6d52f9c0d88d498ddbcb3a":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01XFa9z6ArnP3Uf1dPaTrWSg","name":"grade_answer","input":{"reason":"Sandbox results are in (c100 2/3, c020 2/3, c077 2/3, c162 3/3); grading the collected answers against the rubric so the test results and rubric scores can be compared.","candidate_ids":["c100","c020","c077","c162"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":44368,"output":155,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00454495,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426918814},"91861ea86bac53c77bc396fda55f6f2d3f40e0650e6946cf3930627cf7d7db74":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"yes\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"rationale\":\"The answer separates the prompt from the model by re-running a frozen eval set against old and new versions, checks the harness and parser first, and diffs failures by category to find format or instruction-following regressions. Its fixes are concrete: tightening the output spec, moving constraints, adding examples, and pinning the old version. It also adds failing cases to the held-out set and gates the upgrade on the eval. It omits explicit mention of version-controlling prompts and of output validation, so it is not a perfect match to the expected qualities, but it covers the core of the task.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":700,"output":332,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.000236,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426920861},"81c0f120da6979a84092a440ea2f3bddfebd0e8a97403a67b3380d89b470c0cb":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_017c73zQVxkGNa1r5TdnrzNc","name":"search_candidates","input":{"reason":"Second must-have cluster (m2 evaluation): the first search surfaced only two llm-apps profiles (c060, c131), so I need candidates who evaluate LLM outputs.","query":"evaluation harness for LLM outputs with golden datasets, LLM-as-judge, and regression metrics","mode":"semantic","k":12}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":2809,"output":246,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00043610000000000003,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426923928},"ed5ba3f7147b6d553bf64b2f76826d488f497c9518196bf2e02b7d8c2125212a":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01VsZvcdDKpQHvMK5ghe7uLA","name":"search_candidates","input":{"reason":"Memory notes say pgvector/FAISS/HNSW plus FastAPI profiles were the strongest pool matches, so I'll run a hybrid query naming those tools to cover m1 and m2 together.","query":"pgvector HNSW FAISS vector search with Python FastAPI services on PostgreSQL","mode":"hybrid","k":12}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":1551,"output":304,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0003393,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426923959},"873dd08e2b2f0c2c77318443e41a43a6fdcedaefd6facdfd95f05d3cf63c9663":{"response":{"text":"{\"parts\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"score\":0.8,\"confidence\":0.8,\"note\":\"Separates prompt from model, proposes rerunning a fixed eval set on old and new versions, case-by-case diffs, and several concrete fixes with gating, but never explicitly mentions version-controlling prompts.\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"score\":0.9,\"confidence\":0.85,\"note\":\"Specifies an explicit schema with types and null rules, edge-case few-shot examples, JSON-only output, schema validation with retry and human review, and field-level accuracy on a labelled set.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"score\":0.35,\"confidence\":0.9,\"note\":\"Hidden tests passed 2/3. The string-pattern replace only substitutes the first occurrence, so repeated placeholders stay unfilled, which the rubric flags. It also misses the global-regex callback approach, and string replacement treats '$' sequences in values as special patterns.\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1767,"output":677,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0005152000000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426924265},"8a5fdd04f706621b8fd2fac277392c9f38ddde8b8a41e332e0f86805165b0675":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_018s5FbsHzZMgWkYVRa4965E","name":"get_profile","input":{"candidate_id":"c049","reason":"c049 has the highest semantic score (0.78) and covers pgvector/FAISS and FastAPI, so I need the full text to check for verbatim must-have evidence."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":4019,"output":158,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0005131000000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426925469},"016a2ee8acf82dda52fd435dd4ca3d4d2f07e6d9a4933f5b5e66af193aa539f1":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"no\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"rationale\":\"The answer gestures at comparing old and new outputs but never mentions a fixed evaluation set, case-by-case comparison, or checking for format or instruction-following regressions. Its fix ('make small fixes until the test set passes') risks overfitting to the test set, and it omits concrete remedies such as pinning the model version, adding output validation, or version-controlling prompts. It does not clearly separate prompt problems from model problems.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":421,"output":255,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0001696,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426925966},"6b01ad2f5f21030f3e919819a6e5828f9ee8f7db4e2231ae237f200b46d90252":{"response":{"text":"The evaluation search mostly returned analysts and unrelated backend profiles, so it added little. The two llm-apps profiles from the first search (c060 and c131) are the strongest leads. I'll open both.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01P1wfewjFRhS8mzP1i8qHDJ","name":"get_profile","input":{"reason":"c060 is the top llm-apps match from search 1 and is tagged with both must-haves; need to verify the claims.","candidate_id":"c060"}},{"id":"toolu_013BvmWie42eRJr1tV4CByAN","name":"get_profile","input":{"reason":"c131 is the other llm-apps profile tagged with both must-haves; need to verify the claims.","candidate_id":"c131"}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":5327,"output":392,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0007609000000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426926256},"6e8380ec6a8d62ef6c161c432c433fa552844b9a8eb7d4e9e16d072c32c93eaa":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_016CwBdn1k3dpdiujwNivEUx","name":"cite_evidence","input":{"reason":"c049's profile has project sentences covering all three must-haves and both nice-to-haves, so I'll cite them in one call.","candidate_id":"c049","citations":[{"requirement_id":"m1","quote":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms."},{"requirement_id":"m2","quote":"Wrote the ingestion and query services around the index in FastAPI, with asyncio workers and typed Pydantic models."},{"requirement_id":"m3","quote":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set."},{"requirement_id":"n1","quote":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches."},{"requirement_id":"n2","quote":"Wrote the ingestion and query services around the index in FastAPI, with asyncio workers and typed Pydantic models."}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":5807,"output":444,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0008349000000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426927713},"eed7358e708030616240fa37f825effac3664af4a168c14d56fd3f9ba9e5992f":{"response":{"text":"{\"parts\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"score\":0.15,\"confidence\":0.85,\"note\":\"Mentions checking logs and comparing old and new outputs, but never proposes a fixed evaluation set, format or instruction-following checks, a concrete fix such as pinning or output validation, or version control of prompts.\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"score\":0.1,\"confidence\":0.85,\"note\":\"Gives no explicit schema, missing-value rule, edge-case few-shot examples, or schema validation, retry, or field-level accuracy measurement, so it does not meet the rubric's reliability requirements for a JSON-consuming system.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"score\":0.3,\"confidence\":0.8,\"note\":\"Uses a global regex with a callback, but splits on every '---' instead of the first separator line, and returns undefined for missing keys (rendering 'undefined' instead of leaving the placeholder) and does not guard against inherited object keys; hidden tests pass 2/3.\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1173,"output":519,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0003768,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426928763},"892d24abf73cfbdf8f4c253b491b2e1b6cf7187b2946e335f20f67d9647bf01c":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"yes\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"rationale\":\"The sentence describes building ingestion and query services with FastAPI, asyncio workers, and Pydantic models. FastAPI, asyncio, and Pydantic are Python frameworks/libraries, so this is concrete, hands-on evidence of Python work.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":357,"output":109,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0000902,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426929135},"28c849751ccd7f720e0ebb10a5e5b8cc302d4a7caf7c41960d26128a5441c2ac":{"response":{"text":"Ravi Sharma's profile is mostly a keyword list. The projects are game systems and shaders, with no LLM build or evaluation work described. I'll still run the citation check so the verifier makes the call on the evidence.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01L4Cqmia2q1FibdDTd3NwFr","name":"cite_evidence","input":{"reason":"c060 has keyword-level matches for m1 and m2 only; testing whether any sentence carries real substance before deciding to drop.","candidate_id":"c060","citations":[{"requirement_id":"m1","quote":"Passionate about LLM applications, prompt engineering and RAG."},{"requirement_id":"m2","quote":"Keywords: LLM applications, prompt engineering, RAG, agents, evaluation."}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":7115,"output":527,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0010072,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426929177},"d99bd91b98982e00168457754935738df1d700dae3b233046964613a5e9b6fab":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"no\",\"confidence\":0.85,\"rationale\":\"The sentence states passion and lists topics (LLM applications, prompt engineering, RAG) without describing any built or deployed work. Per the criteria, a 'passionate about' line or bare keyword mention is not concrete evidence of hands-on experience.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":343,"output":105,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00008680000000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426930167},"1dfb08850d8a0f822370000047185431a40edaad55a481140de294b049bd6ebd":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"no\",\"confidence\":0.8,\"rationale\":\"The two changes are specific enough: a grounding instruction to answer only from supplied policy, and few-shot examples of correct refund answers. The verification step is weak. 'Try a few support questions and see if the answers look better' is subjective, with no fixed test set, no known correct answers, no before/after comparison, and no metric for hallucinated policies. The rubric treats skipping a testable verification method as unacceptable.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":484,"output":280,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0001884,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426930480},"447f4f815b987d55c81d8c3d8eb5fe4af78e7ca30a140910db232d8318f4d099":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"yes\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"rationale\":\"The sentence describes building ingestion and query services in FastAPI with typed Pydantic models. That is concrete, hands-on work on API services, and FastAPI is an API framework, so it directly evidences API design.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":357,"output":198,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0001347,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426930538},"ba120a7d32ca42e3f5ef63a5978bf4bbea8e9cb9d173ef336d89061c7138aee9":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"no\",\"confidence\":0.85,\"rationale\":\"The sentence is a bare keyword list that names 'evaluation' alongside other terms but describes no built system, project, or task where evaluation was performed. Per the criteria, a skills enumeration without a built thing is 'no'.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":350,"output":96,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.000083,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426931394},"31585e5087b728d2be6f7a8caf5e4921083ea53fef5ee64729adda3790c00e09":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01UGkbNeLi6k3kLaVJEKcoxc","name":"get_profile","input":{"candidate_id":"c156","reason":"c156 has the next-highest semantic score (0.75) with pgvector/FAISS and FastAPI in the snippet; I need the full profile to find verbatim must-have sentences."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":7542,"output":133,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0008529000000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426931563},"6ddcae0bf48751bcc6c0d2e5272aa9387867958825c50048ef5fccdce26cbeab":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01Xq7nxymLHWL48KcCDrXCrB","name":"drop_candidate","input":{"reason":"c060 claims failed citation: m1 and m2 rest on keyword lists and a \"passionate about\" line, with no built work described.","candidate_id":"c060","why":"Keyword-only skill claims for both must-haves;